  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon shows very little action across \nthe interior CONUS, with high pressure dominating the surface and \nnotable mid/upper shortwaves located over southern California and \nthe Pacific Northwest into the Northern Rockies. A cold start \nnear/below freezing this morning has turned into a brisk fall \nafternoon, with breezy winds out of the southwest cutting a bit \ndeeper than the temperatures in the upper 50s to low 60s would \nsuggest. Winds will become a bit lighter this evening into the \novernight hours, turning increasingly southerly heading into \ntomorrow morning. Fortunately, lows won't be quite as cold \ntonight, bottoming out near to just below 40 degrees, with the \nwind keeping any frost formation and more efficient cooling to a\nminimum. \n\nWinds turn increasingly southerly tomorrow morning, helping to \nusher in warmer temperatures to the area that top out in the \nmid-to-upper 60s. Wind speeds will have a bit of their edge \ntaken off, with gusts being near to 5 mph less than this \nafternoon. Only late in the day will any clouds interrupt our \nclear skies, as a few high-level clouds waft in from the \nsouthwest into the overnight hours.\n\nMonday and Beyond:\n\nSurface high pressure will be hard to dislodge from the eastern half \nof the CONUS, making for poor moisture transport towards the \nregion despite southerly to southwesterly flow through the \nmajority of the upcoming week. A decaying wave will traverse the\nDesert Southwest, losing most of its accompanying moisture as \nit does so. A few of the regional/global models indicate that a \ntrace of moisture is possible as it arrives early Wednesday, but\nwe're not betting on it (less than 10% chance). \n\nOtherwise, temperature trends and increasing winds towards the \nlatter part of the week will be the thing to watch going \nforward. High temperatures in the 60s will turn into 70s by mid-\nweek and strong winds arrive Friday (gusting to 30-40 mph) that \nlast into the weekend. Whatever standing crops remain by this \npoint would lead to a conditional increase in fire danger if \nwidespread rainfall is not ongoing during the afternoon/evenings\nFriday onward.",
